<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fukui Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, with its capital at Fukui. Formed after the abolition of the domains in 1871 and given its present shape in 1881, it covers the old provinces of Echizen and Wakasa. It is known for the Zen temple of Eihei-ji, dinosaur fossils and a concentration of nuclear power stations along its coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fukui-prefecture.jpg" alt="Fukui Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 藤谷良秀(Yoshihide Fujitani) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fukui_plain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
